{
  "slug": "find-median-from-data-stream",
  "title": "Find Median from Data Stream",
  "topic": "heap",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "heap / two-heap running median",
  "statement": "A sensor reports readings one at a time as the elements of nums. Right after each reading arrives, the sensor records the running median of the readings seen so far, using the lower median whenever the count is even: for a sorted list of m readings the lower median is the element at index (m - 1) // 2, counting from zero. The first reading therefore contributes its own value.\n\nReturn the sum of the running medians recorded over all prefixes of nums.\n\nFor example, nums = [1, 2, 3, 4] gives the running medians 1, 1, 2 and 2, so the answer is 6.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200` and `-10000 <= nums[i] <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-10000]",
      "output": "-10000",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [-221, 6635, 9954, -3483]",
      "output": "5972",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "You need the middle value after each insertion, and the middle value depends only on how the readings split into a smaller half and a larger half.",
    "Keep the smaller half in a max-heap and the larger half in a min-heap, and keep their sizes within one of each other.",
    "Define the median as the element at index (m - 1) // 2 of the sorted prefix; then the answer is the top of the max-heap after each balancing move, accumulated over all prefixes."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force keeps the definition literal: after each reading it copies the whole prefix, sorts it, and reads the element at index (m - 1) // 2, which costs O(m log m) per reading and O(n^2 log n) in total. The optimal solution keeps the readings split into a lower half held in a max-heap and an upper half held in a min-heap. Each reading goes into the half it belongs to, one balancing move restores the invariant that the max-heap holds ceil(m/2) elements and the min-heap holds the rest, and the lower median is then always the top of the max-heap.",
    "why_optimal": "A comparison based structure needs Omega(log m) work per insertion to place a new value relative to the ones already stored, so no fully dynamic median costs less than O(n log n) over n readings unless the value range is exploited. The two heaps reach that bound while keeping only O(n) elements alive, and because the lower median convention selects one existing element instead of an average, every recorded median is an integer and the sum stays exact.",
    "pitfalls": "Averaging the two middle values for even sizes and then truncating, which is not the lower median: for nums = [1, 2] the answer is 1, not 1.5 or 2. Failing to rebalance after an insertion, so the top of the max-heap stops being the median. Sending a value to the wrong heap when it ties the current max-heap top, which silently shifts the median. Accumulating only the final median instead of the sum over every prefix, and mishandling the single element prefix, where the lower median is the element itself."
  },
